; NaN and the infinities reach documents — `(sqrt x)` for negative x and
; `(asin x)` for |x| > 1 are documented to produce NaN, and `pow` overflows
; to a signed infinity — but the corpus could not say so, because
; `expected.sjon` has no literal for either and `Expr.Value.equals` is
; IEEE: NaN never equals itself, so a direct `:result` pin could not pass
; even if the literal existed. (The *encoding* of both, into the "nan" /
; "inf" / "-inf" sentinels the wire envelope uses, is unit-tested in
; `wasm_common.zig` — what was missing is that expressions produce them
; at all, and what happens next.)
;
; So this case pins them through the comparisons that are representable.
; Forms 1-4 are the whole of NaN's behaviour under the comparison
; operators: every one is false, including `>` — NaN is not merely
; unequal, it is unordered, so an author cannot bisect for it with a
; comparison and must not assume `(not (< a b))` means `(>= a b)`.
;
; Forms 5/6 are the payoff: `saturate` and `clamp` both turn a NaN into
; 1, not 0 and not NaN. That is a consequence of the clamp being
; @max(lo, @min(hi, v)) and IEEE min/max returning the non-NaN operand,
; it is the same mechanism as the degenerate `smoothstep` in
; `expr-value-smoothing`, and it means NaN does *not* propagate through
; the smoothing functions the way it does through arithmetic. Both agree,
; which is the part worth locking: `saturate` is documented as
; clamp(x, 0, 1) and has a separate implementation.
;
; Forms 8-11 do the same for the infinities: they compare equal to
; themselves, they are ordered against finite values, and `pow` carries
; the sign through an odd exponent.
(sqrt -1.0)
(= (sqrt -1.0) (sqrt -1.0))
(= (sqrt -1.0) 0.0)
(< (sqrt -1.0) 0.0)
(> (sqrt -1.0) 0.0)
(saturate (sqrt -1.0))
(clamp (sqrt -1.0) 0.0 1.0)
(pow 10.0 400.0)
(= (pow 10.0 400.0) (pow 10.0 400.0))
(> (pow 10.0 400.0) 0.0)
(pow -10.0 401.0)
(< (pow -10.0 401.0) 0.0)
